{{!--
  ComponentSpec -- one of fm-web's own two UX-heavy templates (19 §19.1's own "UX-heavy templates"
  row). Renders against 19 §19.2's real TemplateContext shape (project/artifact/kb/spec/inputs/style/
  now/forge), not a framework-specific execution-data object the way packages/templates' own 43
  ADR-output .hbs templates do -- this template never calls kb.get/spec.get (nothing in a component
  spec needs a KB citation or a spec cross-reference), so `context.kb`/`context.spec` are accepted but
  genuinely unused, matching 19 §19.2 rule 2 ("templates are layout and prompting, never logic").

  Only Handlebars built-in helpers are used (#each, #if) -- no custom helper (`{{id}}`, `{{slug}}`,
  `{{required}}`, ...) is registered anywhere in this repository yet (test/output-templates.test.ts's
  own comment, still true as of PLAN-M10.md P3), the same "built-ins only" convention every other real
  .hbs template in this codebase already follows.

  Every triple-stash {{{ }}} is deliberate: a double-stash {{ }} HTML-escapes its value by Handlebars'
  own default, which would corrupt a YAML scalar containing an apostrophe or an ampersand -- this is a
  Markdown/YAML render target, not HTML. Every array field's own {{#each}} carries an {{else}} branch
  rendering a literal "[]", matching packages/templates' own adr-*.md.hbs convention: {{#each}} renders
  nothing at all for an empty array, which would otherwise leave the YAML key with no value (parses as
  null, not []) -- component-spec.schema.json's own array fields do not accept null.
--}}
